*Q: d8-h4**. This puts immediate pressure on White's somewhat exposed king position on f2, threatens h2, and forces White into a defensive posture. With White's king on f2 (not castled to safety), an aggressive queen sortie can be very effective. This move also coordinates well with potential future attacks and prevents White from consolidating their advantage with the strong d5 knight.\n\n**Reason:**\nWhite's king on f2 is vulnerable, and the queen move to h4 creates multiple threats (attacking h2, eyeing the king). This forces White to deal with concrete threats rather than improving their already strong position with the d5 knight. It's the most principled way to fight for the initiative in a roughly equal position.\n\n**Answer: Q: d8-h4**"}
